    {
      "id": "second-wall",
      "year": "52 BC",
      "sortYear": -52,
      "headline": "Then a second wall, facing outward",
      "body": "The riders begin to work. A relief army is coming — tribes converging from across Gaul. Caesar's answer is to build the whole thing again, facing the other way. Twenty-one more kilometres, a complete second ring enclosing the first. His army now holds a corridor between two walls, besieging a fortress while itself besieged. Caesar writes that a quarter of a million men are marching toward him. Historians think far fewer. More, certainly, than he could meet in the open.",
