    {
      "id": "rides-out-alone",
      "year": "52 BC",
      "sortYear": -52,
      "headline": "He rides out alone",
      "body": "The relief army breaks and scatters in the night. The next morning Vercingetorix surrenders. Caesar gives it one flat sentence. Plutarch, writing a century and a half later, adds the full armour, the circle on horseback, the weapons laid at Caesar's feet. Vercingetorix is kept alive in a Roman cell for six years, walked through Rome in Caesar's triumph, and strangled.",
